# Untitled

Efficient and Stable Perovskite Solar Modules Enabled by Inhibited Escape of Volatile Species
The intrinsically weak bonding structure in halide perovskite materials makes components in the thin films volatile, leading to the decomposition of halide perovskite materials. The reactions within the perovskite film are reversible provided that components do not escape the thin films. Here, a holistic approach is reported to improve the efficiency and stability of PSMs by preventing the effusion of volatile components. Specifically, a method for in situ generation of channel barrier layers for perovskite photovoltaic modules is developed. The resulting PSMs attain a certified aperture PCE of \(21.37\%\), and possess remarkable continuous operation stability for maximum power point tracking (MPPT) of \(\mathsf{T}_{90} > 1100\,\mathsf{h}\) in ambient air, and damp heat (DH) tracking of \(\mathsf{T}_{93} > 1400\,\mathsf{h}\).
## 1. Introduction
Reproducibly achieving high efficiency and stability at the module level is critical for the commercialization of perovskite photovoltaic technology. In the past few years, the certified efficiency for small-area laboratory perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has reached \(26.1\%\), and the stability has also been improved substantially as a result of a strongly improved understanding of materials and device properties.[1] The intrinsically weak bonding structure in halide perovskite materials makes components in the thin films volatile, leading to decomposition of halide perovskite materials. The reactions within the perovskite film are reversible
provided that components do not escape the thin films.[2] Qi et al. have analyzed the decomposition pathway of perovskite through quadrupole mass spectrometer, revealing the reversibility of its degradation reaction. Constructing configurations that limit diffusion helps to confine the weakly bonded components in space when subjected to composition segregation and prolongs the lifetime of PSCs. In addition, the instability issue of perovskite solar modules (PSMs), in comparison to that of PSCs with the same device structure, added the reason for being affected by the lateral exposure of perovskite materials in the monolithic interconnection regions. To address this problem, additional ion barrier layer,
such as 2D graphitic carbon nitride nanosheets or ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{\mathrm{x}}\), are used to suppress degradation at the monolithic interconnection regions and improve the stability of module.[3,4] Therefore, protective measures need to be deployed not only at the top of the perovskite thin film, but also at the edge of the sub-cells. Here, we report a holistic approach to improve the efficiency and stability of PSMs by preventing the effusion of volatile components. First, a compact electron transport layer \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{60}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)\) was deposited on top of the perovskite layers to inhibit the vertical escape of volatile species, followed by post-annealing to enhance the redistribution and recrystallization of the perovskite composition. The as-fabricated inverted planar PSCs yield a power conversion efficiency (PCE) as high as \(23.5\%\). We further propose a novel ozone \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)\) groove treatment to in situ construct lead oxide blocking barriers at the edge of the sub-cells. The resulting PSMs attain a certified aperture PCE of \(21.37\%\), and possess remarkable continuous operation stability for maximum power point tracking (MPPT) of \(\mathrm{T}_{90} > 1184\,\mathrm{h}\) in ambient air, and damp heat (DH) tracking of \(\mathrm{T}_{93} > 1400\,\mathrm{h}\).
## 2. Results and Discussion
A schematic illustration of the monolithic interconnection of the perovskite solar module (PSM) is shown in Figure 1a, where a series of sub-cells are separated and interconnected by P1, P2, and P3 scribes. Most of the elements and components in organic-inorganic halide perovskite materials are large and polarized, leading to a soft crystal lattice that makes it susceptible to deformation and vulnerable to heat aging stress.[5] The
degeneration is irreversible once the perovskite components escape and/or react with the metal electrode (Figure 1b), therefore low dimensional perovskite layer stacking,[6–8] inert material layer capping,[9] and compact interfacial layer[10] protection strategies have been adopted to inhibit diffusion of volatile species and preserve the initial stoichiometric ratio of perovskite elements even during long-term aging.[11,12] Nevertheless, these approaches mainly suppress vertical diffusion of components, which is not sufficient for making the solar module stable, as the scribe grooves present a potential lateral effusion risk due to direct exposure of perovskite elements to the metal electrode and/or ambient (Figure 1c).[4,13] Although common external en
capsulation procedures are able to block reactions with the ambient environment, it does not prevent internal reaction due to, e.g., ion migration. Thus, a comprehensive protection method with an internal anti-diffusion layer for a long-term stable behavior of the solar module is needed (Figure 1d).
In this work, vertical diffusion is inhibited by a compact \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer deposited by atomic layer deposition \((\mathrm{ALD-SnO}_{2})\), and lateral diffusion is suppressed by \(\mathrm{PbO}_{x}\) blocking barriers formed in situ by ozone \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)\) treatment of the outer edges of the sub-cells. In order to verify the inhibition of element diffusion, the devices with a structure of ITO/Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA)/perovskite/anti-diffusion
layer/Ag were fabricated and aged at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(200\,\mathrm{h}\) in ambient environment. Time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectroscopy (ToF-SIMS) was applied to probe the element distribution in unprotected devices (without diffusion barriers) and improved devices. As can be seen in Figure 1e, a large amount of \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{+}\) were detected in the Ag layer, and significant \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\) diffusion into the perovskite layer is observed in devices without diffusion barrier layers, indicating potential deterioration.[9] With the improvement due to a dense ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer, almost all \(\mathrm{Pb}^{+}\) are prevented from traversing this layer, and even the notorious \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) migration is largely prevented (Figure 1f). A comparable barrier effect occurs when the perovskite film is covered with a thin \(\mathrm{PbO}_{x}\) film (Figure 1g). Indeed, anti-diffusion layers enable enhanced device stability, while additionally contributing to the photovoltage and fill factor by reducing the current leakage losses. We expect to further utilize these barrier properties to construct a confined microenvironment that allows for film recrystallization by a high-temperature post-annealing procedure without any volatile component loss. It has been reported that maintaining a desired stoichiometry over the entire perovskite thin film is important to reduce vacancy defects, while recrystallization to large grains facilitates healing of non-radiative recombination centers.[14–16]
## 2.1. Perovskite Thin Film Recrystallization without Volatile Species Loss
A sufficient amount of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) is beneficial to the efficiency of PSCs, while excessive \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) can deteriorate the device stability. Therefore, it is necessary to keep the amount of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) constant throughout the whole operational life.[17,18] However, in order to achieve a large grain size, the perovskite thin film is usually heated beyond the decomposition temperature threshold and is therefore prone to suffer from decomposition of the excess \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\). In this work, the PSCs have an inverted structure of indium tin oxide \(\mathrm{ITO/PTAA/Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/(3\mathrm{D}/2\mathrm{D})\) heterojunction perovskite/ \(C_{60}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Ag}\). During the fabrication process of PSCs, the perovskite thin film was heated three times, in total for \({\approx}40\,\mathrm{min}\), i.e., for the formation of the 3D perovskite layer (10 min), for the formation of the 2D perovskite layers \(\left(10\,\mathrm{min}\right)\), and for the deposition of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer \((20\,\mathrm{min})\). In order to prepare stoichiometrically balanced films, heating and/or annealing at relatively low temperature to prevent migration of organic components is feasible, while the as-prepared perovskite thin film is not fully crystallized. In this work, we first applied a low temperature of \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for the preparation of the perovskite thin film and of \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for the deposition of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) compact layer to obtain a stoichiometric perovskite thin film. This is then followed by a high-temperature post-annealing procedure at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with all perovskite components confined in a sealed microenvironment to realize recrystallization growth without loss of volatile species. The construction of a confined microenvironment was realized by applying a dense capping layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) by ALD. A schematic diagram of the secondary crystal regrowth mechanism is shown in Figure 2a, and we denote this process as limited microenvironment post-annealing process (LMAP). The fresh perovskite thin film shows a small \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) peak, and the peak intensity increases to the desired value after the ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) deposition (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The single \(C_{60}\) layer is insufficient to prevent the escape of volatile materials in perovskite layer, and some volatile materials may still loss before forming a dense \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film, leading to an increase in lead iodide content. As expected, a post-annealing process \((150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\,30\,\mathrm{min})\) is able to enhance the crystallinity of both the bare perovskite and the ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) protected perovskite thin films, while the ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) protected perovskite thin film shows stronger (1 1 0) perovskite crystal orientation after post-annealing than the bare perovskite counterpart. More importantly, there is negligible change of the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) peak after the post-annealing process with ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) but an increased peak intensity of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) for perovskite/ \(C_{60}\) thin film, directly illustrating the prevention of escaping components by the capping ALD- \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer. As can be seen in Figure 2b, the perovskite thin film that was prepared without LMAP demonstrates longitudinally layered grains (Figure 2b), which are known to lead to more charge traps distributed at the grain boundaries due to dangling bonds and disordered crystallization. The charge traps may cause the increase of free carrier concentrations that has been reported to lead to narrowing of the depletion region, limiting effective carrier collection. As a contrast, the LMAP-treated counterpart shows a significant improvement in grain size with grain crystals extending throughout the film thickness (Figure 2b, Figure S2, Supporting Information) due to the accelerated recycling of intrinsic components recycling and Ostwald ripening at higher temperature (Figure 2a).[19] We calculated the trap densities of the perovskite layers before and after LMAP. The trap densities were calculated by the space charge limited current (SCLC) method with the equation of \(\eta_{\mathrm{cap}}=(V_{\mathrm{LMT}}\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}})/\epsilon\mathrm{I}^{2}\), where \(V_{\mathrm{LMT}}\) is the trap-fill limit voltage, L and \(\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\) are the thickness (610 nm for W/O LMAP, 620 nm for W/ LMAP) and relative dielectric constant of perovskite active layer, \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum permittivity, and e is the electron charge. The trap density is reduced from \(2.43\times10^{15}\) to \(1.87\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) (Figure S3, Supporting Information).
