perovskite solar cells (PSCs) fabricated with LMAP treatment show an overall improvement in the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum,[22] contributing to an increase of the integrated \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) from 21.4 to \(22.3\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 2g). The as-fabricated best-performing PSCs demonstrate a PCE of \(23.21\%\), with a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(24.05\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.14\,\mathrm{V}\), and an FF of 0.85, which demonstrates a significant improvement in efficiency with respect to the control device (Figure 2f; Table S2, Supporting Information). In addition, our PSCs with LMAP treatment deliver a narrower efficiency distribution, which is a prerequisite for fabricating efficient PSMs (Figure S4, Supporting Information).
2.2. In Situ Construction of \(\mathbf{PbO_{x}}\) Blocking Barriers at the Edge of the Sub-Cells
Inspired by the formation of strong chemical bonding of layers, e.g., \(\mathrm{PbSO_{4}}\),[23] \(\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}\),[23] or PbS,[24] on perovskite surfaces that are able to avoid the formation of iodine interstitial defects and ion migration (or diffusion), we intended to construct a blocking barrier on the walls of the scribe grooves between adjacent sub-cells. Because conformal deposition of thin films on vertical scribe groove surfaces is difficult, deposited anti-diffusion materials filling the grooves may lead to wide dead areas while vertical deposition of dense layers may increase the contact resistance between the metal electrode and the ITO layer.[3,4] Therefore, in this second aspect of our holistic approach to inhibit perovskite thin film degeneration due to ion migration, we propose the in situ construction of lead oxide blocking barriers by \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) gas treatment. We show that the exposed perovskite material to the metal electrode and to ambient as a result of laser scribing can effectively be protected against lateral ion diffusion.
tance between the metal electrode and the ITO layer.[3,4] Therefore, in this second aspect of our holistic approach to inhibit perovskite thin film degeneration due to ion migration, we propose the in situ construction of lead oxide blocking barriers by \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) gas treatment. We show that the exposed perovskite material to the metal electrode and to ambient as a result of laser scribing can effectively be protected against lateral ion diffusion.
Initially, an oversaturated \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment was carried out on a perovskite film surface to evaluate the final reaction product. The XRD pattern shows the preferred orientation peak at \(13.06^{\circ}\), which can be associated with the lead halide or lead oxide (Figure S5, Supporting Information). X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were performed to certify the formation of \(\mathrm{PbO_{x}}\), as the PbI4 and O1s peaks closely match those of PbO film prepared by evaporation of PbO powder (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Interestingly, deep defects due to \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) are readily oxidized to \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) (Figure S7, Supporting Information), and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) is also oxidized to \(\mathrm{PbO_{x}}\), as evidenced by Figure S8 (Supporting Information) where the XRD peak of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) clearly decreases with prolonging \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment. Here, we propose the mechanism of reaction process as follows:
\[
C_{\mathrm{S_{0.05}}}(FA_{\mathrm{0.9}}MA_{\mathrm{0.1}})_{\mathrm{0.95}}Pb(I_{\mathrm{0.9}}Br_{\mathrm{0.1}})_{3}\rightarrow\mathrm{PbI}_{2}+(CsMAFA)I_{\mathrm{y}}Br_{\mathrm{z}}
\]
\[
\mathrm{PbI}_2+\mathrm{O}_3\rightarrow\mathrm{PbO}_x+\mathrm{I}_2\downarrow+\mathrm{O}_2\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{Pb}+\mathrm{O}_3\rightarrow\mathrm{PbO}_x
\]
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image in Figure 3 clearly highlights the formation of perovskite \(/\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) hybrid thin films with a different lattice constant due to the \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment on the perovskite thin film surface (Figure 3a). For the laser scribing groove treatment, a stratification can be observed in the cross-sectional SEM images (Figure 3b), indicating that a \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) blocking layer is formed in situ. We verified the absence of reactivity between \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}\) by monitoring the change in XRD patterns of the perovskite/Ag and \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ag}\) films. Severe degeneration occurs in the perovskite film, as it significantly decomposes as is clear from the large amount of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) after 20 days of storage in ambient environment and it further decomposes to an amorphous state after 40 days of storage. In contrast, the \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) thin film obtained by \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment exhibits only slight XRD changes after aging 40 days in ambient environment (Figure 3c). Moreover, in order to verify the diffusion barrier effect of \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) at the edges of the sub-cells, we tracked the changes in electrode resistance in a configuration as illustrated in the schematic diagram of the device in Figure S9 (Supporting Information). The reaction at the interface of the perovskite and the \(\mathrm{Ag}\) electrode will reduce the conductivity of the electrode. For comparison, the conductivity of the electrode stays constant during \(500\,\mathrm{min}\) by the
presence of \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) (Figure 3d) while the resistance of the sample without \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment (and thus without \(\mathrm{PbO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) barrier) increase to \(10^{9}\,\Omega\).
It should be noted that improving the stability of PSMs requires the combined protection of P2 and P3 scribes. Compared with previous reports where only P2 protection could be achieved,[8,20] our proposed ozone gas treatment strategy is able to realize simultaneous P2 and P3 protection. Specially, the \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment of the P3 scribe leads to additional benefits, namely, reduced shunt current leakage that is conventionally due to the formation of metal burrs (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The metal debris has a high specific surface area, which facilitates its oxidation, which in turn reduces the conductivity. To better understand the impact of this effect, we use an equivalent circuit model to simulate the PSM with different unintentional interconnection resistors \(\left(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{U}}\right)\) between adjacent sub-cells (Figure S11a, Supporting Information). If the metal burrs cause a leakage with a sufficiently small \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{U}}\) \((<\!1000\,\Omega)\), the curve will become abnormal, and when R becomes large enough (over \(1000\,\Omega\)), the unintentional shunt is avoided and the curve will return to normal (Figure S11b). The change in the shape of the \(J{-}V\) curves with \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{U}}\) values in the range of \(50{-}10\ 000\ \Omega\) is consistent with the experimental case without \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment (Figure S11c, Supporting Information). Our experiments show a consistent trend
of significant improvements in FF of the PSM when using an \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment of metal debris otherwise causing shunting pathways. Thus, the proposed method is beneficial for improving the production yield of PSMs.
We use micro-view electroluminescence (EL) to observe the expansion of the dead area due to the aging of P2 and P3 scribes. Surprisingly, spontaneous ion diffusion occurs rapidly when the device is transferred to air without protection, as can be concluded from the fact that the EL luminescence is quenched around the dead area even for a fresh-made PSM (Figure 3e). The area of quenched EL luminescence becomes significantly larger after storage in air at \({\approx}60\%\) humidity for 48h (Figure 3f). In case the sample has undergone \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment, the initial reaction is prevented and the reaction caused by prolonged exposure to air is considerable suppressed (Figure 3g,h). The \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment thus provides adequate protection during the time until the modules are encapsulated in a mass production process.
## 2.3. Perovskite Solar Module Photovoltaic Performance
The PSMs were fabricated by selectively combining either the proposed LMAP or both the LMAP and \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment methods combined. The statistical histogram of PCE is shown in Figure 4a. The LMAP treatment alone as well as the combined \(\mathrm{LMAP}/\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment are beneficial to the efficiency of the PSMs. Typical \(J{-}V\) curves of the PSMs obtained using the different processing methods are shown in Figure 4b and the photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table 1. Significant improvement in \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) is
obtained for the LMAP-treated PSMs, which is consistent with the results on PSCs. For additional \(\mathrm{O}_{3}\) treatment, the FF and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of PSMs demonstrate a simultaneous increase due to the reduced internal resistance in the electrode and the inhibited undesired recombination at the edges of sub-cells.
We sent our champion PSM (Figure S12, Supporting Information) to the Chinese national PV industry measurement and testing center (NPVM) for independent certification. The certified PCE under reverse scan ( \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) to \(I_{\mathrm{SC}})\) is \(21.37\%\) (Figure 4c, Figure S13, Supporting Information), the aperture area of the PSM is \(12.84~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) , and an \(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(47.15~\mathrm{mA}\) , \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(7.195~\mathrm{V},\) and FF of \(80.89\%\) is observed. As determined by optical microscopy, the width of the dead area is \(468.3\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) , implying a geometric filling factor (GFF) as high as \(92.20\%\) at the chosen sub-cell width of \(6~\mathrm{mm}\) . As a result, the PSM shows a PCE of \(23.18\%\) for the active area of \(11.84~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) . The PCE of the module obtained from
the forward scan (\(I_{\mathrm{SC}}\) to \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\)) is \(20.48\%\), giving a relatively small hysteresis–index of \(4.16\%\), which is consistent with that of the PSC. More importantly, the champion PSM was also certified to have a constant PCE of \(20.56\%\) over \(300\,s\) at the maximum power point (MPP) (Figure 4d, Figure S14, Supporting Information). The performance of our PSMs is currently one of the highest efficiency in the world (Figure S15, Supporting Information). In order to evaluate the operational stability of our PSMs in which the blocking treatments where implemented to prevent the escape of perovskite components, we encapsulated the PSM and probed the behavior under long-term MPPT tracking. The target PSM retained \(90\%\) of its initial PCE \(\left(\mathrm{T}_{90}\right)\) for \(1184\,\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 4e). We also conducted damp heat tests on our perovskite module, it still retained \(93\%\) of its initial efficiency after \(1416\,\mathrm{h}\) of aging (Figure 4f). Those performance of both stability and efficiency provide ample opportunity to satisfy critical requirements for commercialization of perovskite modules.
## 3. Conclusion
In this work, we propose a scheme to achieve efficient and stable perovskite photovoltaic modules. Specifically, \(\mathrm{PbO_{x}}\) materials are directionally generated by selective growth using a gas-solid reaction method. And we have confirmed that \(\mathrm{PbO_{x}}\) can be good barrier and passivation layer. As a result, our PSMs achieved a certification efficiency of \(21.37\%\) and demonstrated excellent stability. This scheme provides a good idea for achieving efficient and stable perovskite photovoltaic modules in industry.
## 4. Experimental Section
Chemicals: Lead iodide \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), Purity \(>\!99.99\%\) ) and lead bromide \((\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}\), Purity \(>\!99.99\%\) ) were purchased from Xi'an eLight Co., Ltd., cesium iodide (Csl, Purity \(>\!99.9\%\) ), methylamine bromide (MABr, Purity \(\geq99.5\%\) ), poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine (PTAA, Purity \(\geq99.5\%\) ), C60 (Purity \(\geq99.5\%\) ), 2-phenylethanamine iodide (PEAI, Purity \(\geq99.5\%\) ), were purchased from Xi'an Polymer Light Co., Ltd. Formamidinium iodide (FAI, Purity \(\geq99.99\%\) ) was purchased from Greatcell. Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, Purity \(\geq99.9\%\) ), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, Purity \(\geq99.8\%\) ), chlorobenzene (Purity \(\geq99\%\) ), propan-2-ol(IPA, Purity \(\geq99.5\%\) ) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich.
Precursor Solution Preparation: The perovskite precursor solution of \(\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}\left(\mathsf{F A}_{0.9}\mathsf{M A}_{0.1}\right)_{0.95}\mathsf{P b}\left(\mathsf{l}_{0.9}\mathsf{B r}_{0.1}\right)\) was prepared by mixing solutions of FAPbl3, \(\mathsf{M A P b B r}_{3}\) , and Csl in a volume ratio of 52:8:3. The \(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}\) solution with a concentration of \(1.5~\mathsf{m}\) was prepared by adding \(691.5\ m g\ P b1_{2}\) and 237.3 mg FAI into \(1\;\mathsf{m L}\) DMF and DMSO (volume ratio was 4:1) mixture solvent. The \(\mathsf{M A P b B r}_{3}\) solution with a concentration of \(1.5~\mathsf{M}\) was prepared by adding \(165~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) and \(46.35\ m g\ M A B r\) into \(300~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) DMF/DMSO mixture solvent mentioned above. The Csl solution with a concentration of \(1.5~\mathsf{M}\) was prepared by dissolving \(58.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{Csl}\) into \(150\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) DMSO solvent.
Thin Film Deposition and Device Fabrication: The ITO-coated glass was washed in detergent (2 vol% Hellmanex III), deionized water, and ethanol. After drying under \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) flow, the substrate was subsequently treated in ultraviolet ozone for \(10\,\mathsf{m i n}\) to raise the hydrophilicity and further clean the substrate surface. The hole transporting layer of PTAA was prepared by coating the solution in chlorobenzene \((1.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) at 4000 rpm for 30 s and then heating at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathsf{m i n}\). The \(\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) nanoparticle layer was deposited to modify the surface energy of the PTAA layer using the original solution diluted 1:50 in IPA and spin coating at rpm for \(30\,\mathsf{s}\). For the perovskite thin film with small-size substrate \((2.5\times2.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) or large-size substrate \((5\times5.6\;\mathrm{cm}^{2})\), the perovskite solution was spin-coated with a two-step procedure at 2000 rpm for 3 s and 4000 rpm for \(35\,\mathsf{s}\). During the
second step, 100 or \(300\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ethyl acetate was dropped onto the spinning substrate \(10\,\mathsf{s}\) prior to the end of the step, followed by an annealing procedure at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathsf{m i n}\). PEAI dissolved in IPA with a concentration of \(1\;\mathsf{m g}\;\mathsf{m L}^{-1}\) was spin-coated onto the perovskite thin film surface at 5000 rpm for \(30\,\mathsf{s}\), followed by annealing at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathsf{m i n}\). The \(\mathsf{C}_{60}\) film was evaporated to a thickness of \(40\,\mathsf{n m}\). The \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) film was deposited by atomic layer deposition with tin(IV) dimethylamide as Sn source and \(\mathsf{P b O}\) as O source at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 200 cycles. For the LMAP-treated device, the sample was transferred to a \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\)-filled glovebox and post-annealed at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\,\mathsf{m i n}\). Finally, the Ag electrode was thermally evaporated to a thickness of \(120\,\mathsf{n m}\).
Laser Scribing Procedure: The P1 isolation lines were scribed onto the ITO film by Helios fluor laser processing system at a wavelength of 5164 nm and minimal pulse duration of \(30\,\mathsf{n s}\). The Helios VIS laser with a wavelength of \(532\,\mathsf{n m}\) and minimal pulse duration of \(0.4\,\mathsf{n s}\) was applied for P2 and P3 scribing by means of induced ablation model. The P2 scribing procedure was carried out after the deposition of ALD- \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) film and the P3 was scribed after the deposition of metal electrode.
\(\mathsf{O}_{3}\) Treatment on Laser Scribing Grooves: The samples were placed in a chamber, in the presence of an ozone flow of 100 Sccm, and a certain amount of nitrogen was introduced to maintain the pressure at \(\approx\!100\,\mathsf{P a}\). During the whole process, the temperature of the chamber was between \(20\,\mathsf{C}\) and \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the treatment lasted for \(15\,\mathsf{m i n}\).
Characterization: XRD patterns were performed by Bruker D8 Advance using \(\mathsf{C u}\,\mathsf{K}\alpha\) radiation at \(40\,\mathsf{k V}\) and \(40\,\mathsf{m A}\). The morphologies of the films and solar cells were measured by SEM (FEI Apreo LoVac). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed by PHI 5000 VersaProbe III with a monochromatic Al \(\mathsf{K}\alpha\) X-ray source with X-ray beam size of \(200\,\mathsf{u m}\). TEM imaging was performed on a JEOL JEM-3200E transmission electron microscope in scanning mode at an accelerating voltage of \(200\,\mathsf{k V}\). The C–V curves was collected from electrochemical workstation (ZAHNER GIMPS, Germany). The TRPL spectra were recorded by using an Edinburgh Instruments FLS1000 PL spectrometer. The micro-view electroluminescence (EL) images were detected by a home-assembled electroluminescence instrument equipped with a CCD camera (Dnyana 401D).
